  "torus_obstruction": "In an integral torus-graded algebra with invariant normalizedh and zero eventual pure radical on the weight-zero subalgebra,any nonzero jointly pure eigenvector generators have weights whose convex hull excludes0. Otherwise a nonzero balanced product is neutral and pure,a contradiction. Every finitely generated torus-stable augmented pure algebra is strictly separated and marked-safe. On BOTH exceptional quotients this forbids the proposed nonzero opposite-weight jointly pure ORIGINAL invariant pair in all degrees and after automorphisms,for ANY marked radial law. Joint purity is the failed hypothesis;individual mixed pure phases can retain cancellations and are not excluded.",
  "exceptional_marker_obstruction": "For ANY actual homogeneous four-Gaussian pure invariant lift,EVERY ambient Gaussian marker of degree<8 on2O or<12 on2I has all positive marked moments zero. Low-degree Reynolds averages are only determinant powers,then radial integration reduces to the zero pure moments. Hence the old quadratic bad marker cannot transfer even if the proposed generators are noninvariant. Separately,the literal radial law(2n)!/2^n for homogeneous invariant S,T and any fixed polynomial marker would requiredegS+degT4,whereas genuine invariant joint-pure generators have degrees>=8/12 each. Other gradings/nonjoint presentations/new moment laws still require actual identities;neither full exceptional MZ theorem is decided.",
  "sphere_denominators": "For every dimensionN>=1 and even sphere monomialx^(2alpha),m=sumalpha,the exact normalized moment isprod(1/2)_alpha/(N/2)_m. The explicit integer4^m*lcm(1,...,2m+N)^(N+2) clears EVERY such denominator. Separate even/odd-N factorial formulas and floor-carry bounds prove this uniformly. L_(2k) dividesL_k*binomial(2k,k),giving L_k<=16^k and exponential denominators for fixedN. No prime-number,Gauss-Manin regularity or G-function theorem imported.",
  "homogeneous_growth": "For EVERY N>=1,nonzero algebraic-coefficient homogeneous phaseP of degreeD>=1,and ANY fixed algebraic polynomial markerQ,putA_n=E[QP^n],k_n=ceil(Dn/2),R_n=2^k_n*(N/2)_k_n. There areC,n0 with each n>=n0 eitherA_n0 orC^-n<=|A_n/R_n|<=C^n. Compact sphere bounds all conjugates,explicit exponential denominator clearing and a nonzero algebraic norm prove the lower bound. Every infinite nonzero subsequence haslog|A_n|/(nlogn)->D/2. The normalized ordinary series is polynomial or has finite positive radius;entire iff the marked tail is eventuallyzero. No pointwise lower bound for arbitrary complex coefficients is asserted.",
  "exact_degree": "For arbitrary COMPLEX homogeneousP of positive degreeD,in ANY Gaussian dimension,and any fixed finite polynomial markerQ,an EXACT all-index lawE[QP^n]=c^n*(ell*n+b)!,c nonzero,ell,b nonnegative integers,forcesD=2ell. More generally an exactly prescribed rational non-eventually-zero sequence with finite logarithmic ordertheta forcesD/2=theta. Keep all moment equations andc inverse in a finite-typeQ-algebra:properness and classical weak Nullstellensatz supply an algebraic solution,then the growth theorem forces the degree. This is not a finite-prefix specialization argument or a complex pointwise lower estimate. Examples(ZW)^ell show the necessary degree is attainable.",
  "implementation": "Exact normalized fixed-marker Wick calculator,small sphere/LCM and number-field norm controls,a necessary exact-factorial degree gate,and balanced-weight certificates are implemented. The degree gate does NOT verify an all-index law or identify invariance. No numerical asymptotic fit,prime campaign,generic invariant/marker solver,or full exceptional MZ decision is implemented.",
